#---
# purpose: "A dispatch test reads a cons cell's first element, so lists of different element types are different questions"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# fz-kdt.107 step 3. A list test used to see empty-or-cons and nothing else,
# so `[integer]` and `[:ok | :err]` were one and the same question and the
# clause written first took every value: this printed `1 1 2 2`, and which
# answer you got was which clause you wrote first. That is the same defect
# `dispatch_annotated_tuple_tag_clauses` pins for tuple tags, one axis over.
#
# The test now asks a cons cell's HEAD its own question, so the two clauses
# are two questions and both orders print the same two lines.
#
# ## The head is a ONE-SIDED FILTER, and the last four lines are why
#
# A list type is homogeneous -- one element type for the whole list -- so a
# head OUTSIDE the question proves the value outside the surface, while a head
# INSIDE it proves nothing about the tail no test reads. Rejection is exact;
# acceptance is not.
#
# The `Enum.all?` lines settle one `List.reduce_while_cont/3` callsite with the
# three arms that named this ticket:
#
#     A   [:false | :true]        Enum.all?([true, false])
#     B   [int | :ok | :true]     Enum.all?([true, 1, :ok])
#     C   [int]                   Enum.all?([1, 2])
#
# A and C have DISJOINT heads, which is a real separation. B and C OVERLAP at
# the head (both admit an int) and differ only in a tail, so that pair is
# erasing and the seat falls back to surface coverage: B holds everything C
# does, so B is seated first and `[1, :ok]` -- the fourth line -- reaches the
# body that named it instead of the one that reads every element as an int.
# Before this (measured at f69c5ea26): the callsite settled FOUR arms and
# fz-kdt.118's drop removed the two narrower ones -- C was not a destination,
# so no arrival could seat it first. What DID abort was the settled order and
# arm seeds 1/2/3/4/6: an int element read by the [:false | :true] body
# (`fz_unbox_atom`, exit 134 on `run` and `build`). `interp` survived on its
# dynamic tags, which is why this is a three-path-parity fixture and not a
# stdout one.
#
# What no head can reach is a pair whose heads overlap and whose surfaces are
# incomparable; `enum_predicate_search` carries the corpus's two, they keep
# arrival order, and fz-kdt.131 owns them.
fn kind(xs :: [integer]), do: 1
fn kind(xs :: [:ok | :err]), do: 2

fn dnik(xs :: [:ok | :err]), do: 2
fn dnik(xs :: [integer]), do: 1

fn pick(n) do
  if n > 0 do
    [n, n]
  else
    [:ok, :err]
  end
end

fn main() do
  dbg(kind(pick(1)))
  dbg(kind(pick(0)))
  dbg(dnik(pick(1)))
  dbg(dnik(pick(0)))

  dbg(Enum.all?([true, false]))
  dbg(Enum.all?([true, 1, :ok]))
  dbg(Enum.all?([1, 2]))
  dbg(Enum.all?([1, :ok]))
end
